(window.DETAIL_C = window.DETAIL_C || {})["chem-12-10"] = {"chem-12-10-m01":{"idea":"Alkyl halides are named by where the halogen sits: on a saturated carbon, a ring, or a C=C carbon.","steps":["In CH₂=CH–Cl, the carbon holding Cl is one of the two carbons of the C=C bond.","A halogen on a doubly bonded (sp²) carbon makes a vinylic halide.","The molecule is built on an alkene, so it is a haloalkene."],"others":"A benzylic halide has the halogen on a carbon next to a benzene ring, and there is no ring here. An allylic halide has the halogen on the carbon next to C=C, not on it. An alkyl halide has only single bonds.","trap":"Allylic means next to the double bond. Vinylic means on the double bond.","answer":"a vinylic halide, which is a haloalkene"},"chem-12-10-m02":{"idea":"The halogen in chlorobenzene is joined directly to the benzene ring.","steps":["In C₆H₅Cl, Cl is bonded to a carbon that is part of the aromatic ring.","That ring carbon is sp² hybridised.","A halogen on an aromatic ring carbon makes an aryl halide."],"others":"Allylic halides need a C=C bond with the halogen on the next carbon. Benzylic halides have the halogen on a side-chain carbon, and chlorobenzene has no side chain. Alkyl halides have the halogen on an sp³ carbon, not on the ring.","trap":"Aryl: halogen on the ring. Benzylic: halogen on the carbon next to the ring.","answer":"aryl halides, as the halogen is on an sp² carbon of the ring"},"chem-12-10-m03":{"idea":"An alkyl halide is primary, secondary or tertiary by the number of carbons joined to the carbon that holds the halogen.","steps":["Look at the carbon bonded to Br in (CH₃)₃C–Br.","It is joined to three CH₃ groups, so it has three carbon neighbours.","Three carbon neighbours means a tertiary alkyl halide."],"others":"Two carbon neighbours would make it secondary, but here there are three. The carbon has no hydrogen at all, so three hydrogens is wrong. One carbon neighbour would make it primary.","trap":"Count carbon neighbours, not hydrogens.","answer":"tertiary alkyl halide, as the carbon with Br is joined to three other carbons"},"chem-12-10-m04":{"idea":"IUPAC names use the longest chain, with the halogen as a prefix and the lowest possible number.","steps":["The chain CH₃–CH–CH₃ has three carbons, so the parent is propane.","Br is on the middle carbon, which is carbon 2.","The name is 2-bromopropane."],"others":"Isopropyl bromine is a wrong mix of common name and element name; it is not IUPAC. 2-bromoethane would need a two-carbon chain. 1-bromopropane has Br on an end carbon.","trap":"Number the chain so the halogen gets the smaller number.","answer":"2-bromopropane"},"chem-12-10-m05":{"idea":"Name the parent chain first, then list the substituents in alphabetical order.","steps":["Find the longest chain through the carbon that holds Cl: it has three carbons, so propane.","Both Cl and CH₃ are on carbon 2.","Alphabetical order puts chloro before methyl.","The name is 2-chloro-2-methylpropane."],"others":"2-methyl-2-chlorobutane picks a four-carbon chain that does not exist here. 1-chloro-2,2-dimethylethane is not a valid chain. tert-chloro-butane is not a proper name at all.","trap":"The longest chain here is three carbons, not four.","answer":"2-chloro-2-methylpropane"},"chem-12-10-m06":{"idea":"A good preparation method gives a pure product with little work.","steps":["The reaction is R–OH + SOCl₂ → R–Cl + SO₂ + HCl.","SO₂ and HCl are gases, so they leave the flask.","Only the alkyl chloride stays behind, nearly pure.","This is why SOCl₂ is preferred."],"others":"SO₂ and HCl are not liquids that stay mixed, and they are not solids to filter. SOCl₂ does not work with alkanes; it needs an –OH group to react.","trap":"Gaseous by-products make the work-up easy.","answer":"the other products, SO₂ and HCl, are gases that leave the mixture"},"chem-12-10-m07":{"idea":"The Lucas test sorts alcohols by how fast they form an alkyl chloride.","steps":["Lucas reagent is concentrated HCl with anhydrous ZnCl₂.","The reaction goes through a carbocation.","A tertiary carbocation is the most stable, so tertiary alcohols react fastest.","The alkyl chloride is insoluble, so the solution turns cloudy."],"others":"AlCl₃ is not the Lucas catalyst, and tertiary alcohols would not be slowest. Dilute HCl is too weak. H₂SO₄ is not part of the reagent, and primary alcohols are the slowest, not the fastest.","trap":"Order of speed: tertiary, then secondary, then primary.","answer":"concentrated HCl with anhydrous ZnCl₂, and tertiary alcohols react with it fastest"},"chem-12-10-m08":{"idea":"A reaction can be pushed to completion by removing one product as a solid.","steps":["The reaction R–Cl + NaI ⇌ R–I + NaCl is reversible.","NaI dissolves in dry acetone, but NaCl does not.","NaCl separates out as a solid and leaves the mixture.","By Le Chatelier's principle, the equilibrium shifts right."],"others":"NaI is the dissolved reactant, so it is soluble in acetone. Acetone is only the solvent and is not an oxidising agent. The alkyl iodide stays in solution and does not separate.","trap":"The driving force is the insoluble NaCl, not the alkyl iodide.","answer":"NaCl is not soluble in acetone, and it comes out of the solution"},"chem-12-10-m09":{"idea":"The Swarts reaction makes alkyl fluorides by swapping Cl or Br for F.","steps":["Direct fluorination with F₂ is too violent to control.","In the Swarts reaction, an alkyl chloride or bromide is heated with a metal fluoride.","AgF, Hg₂F₂, CoF₂ or SbF₃ are used.","The fluoride replaces the chlorine or bromine."],"others":"An alcohol with HX and ZnCl₂ is the Lucas method for chlorides. NaI in acetone is the Finkelstein reaction, which gives iodides. An alkane with F₂ in sunlight would be a violent reaction and is not Swarts.","trap":"Finkelstein gives iodides. Swarts gives fluorides.","answer":"an alkyl chloride or bromide with a metal fluoride such as AgF or Hg₂F₂"},"chem-12-10-m10":{"idea":"For the same alkyl group, a heavier halogen gives stronger van der Waals forces.","steps":["The alkyl group is the same, so only the halogen changes.","Going from Cl to Br to I, the atom gets bigger and heavier.","A bigger electron cloud is more polarisable.","So the van der Waals forces are strongest in CH₃CH₂I, and its boiling point is the highest."],"others":"The C–Cl bond is the most polar, but size matters more here, so the chloride boils lowest. The three do not have the same boiling point. Bond strength does not decide the boiling point, and C–Br is not the strongest bond anyway.","trap":"Boiling point: R–Cl < R–Br < R–I.","answer":"CH₃CH₂I, as the molecule is the largest and the most polarisable"},"chem-12-10-m11":{"idea":"Among isomers, a more branched molecule has a lower boiling point.","steps":["n-Butyl bromide is a long chain. tert-Butyl bromide is nearly a ball.","A ball has less surface area to touch other molecules.","So the van der Waals forces are weaker.","Weaker forces mean a lower boiling point."],"others":"Polarity is not what separates them, and a more polar molecule would not have weaker forces. The two isomers have the same molecular mass. A less compact molecule would have a higher boiling point, the opposite of tert-butyl bromide.","trap":"Same formula: more branches, lower boiling point.","answer":"its molecule is more compact, so the surface area is smaller and the forces are weaker"},"chem-12-10-m12":{"idea":"Dissolving needs new attractions that pay back for the ones broken.","steps":["Water molecules are held together by strong hydrogen bonds.","To dissolve, the halide must break some of these bonds.","Alkyl halides cannot form hydrogen bonds with water.","The new attractions are weaker, so little dissolves."],"others":"The molecules are polar, because the C–X bond is polar, so non-polar is wrong. Alkyl halides do not react completely with water at once. Density decides floating, not solubility.","trap":"Polar does not always mean soluble in water.","answer":"they cannot form hydrogen bonds with water to make up for the bonds broken in water"},"chem-12-10-m13":{"idea":"A chiral carbon is joined to four different groups.","steps":["Check carbon 2 of each compound.","In 2-bromobutane, carbon 2 has H, Br, CH₃ and C₂H₅. All four are different.","In 2-bromopropane, two groups are CH₃, which are the same.","In bromoethane and 1-bromobutane, the carbon with Br has two hydrogens."],"others":"Bromoethane and 1-bromobutane have CH₂Br, with two hydrogens, so no chiral carbon. 2-bromopropane has two identical CH₃ groups on carbon 2, so it is also not chiral.","trap":"Two same groups on a carbon means it is not chiral.","answer":"2-bromobutane, CH₃CH(Br)CH₂CH₃"},"chem-12-10-m14":{"idea":"A racemic mixture has equal amounts of two enantiomers.","steps":["The (+) and (−) enantiomers rotate light by the same angle.","They rotate it in opposite directions.","In equal amounts, the two rotations cancel.","So the mixture shows no net rotation."],"others":"Each molecule is chiral and has no plane of symmetry. Enantiomers rotate in opposite directions, not the same one. The molecules are chiral, so saying they are not chiral is wrong.","trap":"Cancelling between molecules, not symmetry inside a molecule.","answer":"the rotation of one enantiomer is cancelled by the other, as they are in equal amounts"},"chem-12-10-m15":{"idea":"Enantiomers are mirror images that cannot be placed on top of each other.","steps":["They have the same molecular formula and the same bonds.","Their melting point, boiling point and density are the same.","They turn plane polarised light by equal angles.","The directions are opposite, one (+) and one (−)."],"others":"Enantiomers share a molecular formula. Their melting and boiling points are equal. Equal angles in the same direction would make them the same compound, not mirror images.","trap":"Only the direction of rotation differs.","answer":"rotate plane polarised light by equal angles in opposite directions"},"chem-12-10-m16":{"idea":"SN2 means substitution, nucleophilic, with two species in the one rate-determining step.","steps":["The nucleophile attacks the carbon from the back, opposite to the leaving group.","The bond to the nucleophile forms as the old bond breaks, so it is one step.","The three other groups flip over like an umbrella.","The result is an inversion of configuration (Walden inversion)."],"others":"SN2 is not a two-step process. Retention would need attack from the front, and a racemic product comes from the SN1 pathway. One step with retention is wrong, because back attack gives inversion.","trap":"SN2: one step, inversion. SN1: two steps, racemisation.","answer":"in one step, with an inversion of the configuration"},"chem-12-10-m17":{"idea":"SN1 goes through a flat carbocation, so the stereochemistry is lost.","steps":["Step 1 is slow: the halide ion leaves and a carbocation forms.","The carbocation is planar (sp²).","The nucleophile can attack from either face.","Both enantiomers form in about equal amounts, so the product is largely racemised."],"others":"One open side is not true; both faces are open. Inversion alone is the SN2 result. A carbocation does not hold its shape, as it is flat and attacked from either side.","trap":"Largely racemised, not fully, since some inversion can occur.","answer":"racemised, as the intermediate carbocation is planar and is attacked from both sides"},"chem-12-10-m18":{"idea":"SN2 is slowed by crowding at the carbon that is attacked.","steps":["The nucleophile must reach the back of the carbon.","CH₃Br has only hydrogens there, so it is the easiest to attack.","Each alkyl group added makes the back more crowded.","So the order is CH₃Br > primary > secondary > tertiary."],"others":"Tertiary being fastest describes SN1, where carbocation stability matters. Bulky groups do not help back attack; they block it. The four are not equal, because the crowding differs.","trap":"SN2 order is the reverse of SN1 order.","answer":"CH₃Br > primary > secondary > tertiary, as bulky groups block the back attack"},"chem-12-10-m19":{"idea":"The speed of SN1 depends on how fast the carbocation forms.","steps":["The slow step is the loss of X⁻ to give a carbocation.","Alkyl groups push electrons to the positive carbon and stabilise it.","A tertiary carbocation is the most stable, and a primary one the least.","So the order is tertiary > secondary > primary."],"others":"Bulk does not help SN1; the tertiary halide is actually the most crowded. An open back is the SN2 argument. A primary carbocation is the least stable, not the most.","trap":"SN1 loves stable carbocations: tertiary first.","answer":"tertiary > secondary > primary, as the tertiary carbocation is the most stable"},"chem-12-10-m20":{"idea":"The solvent and base decide between elimination and substitution.","steps":["In alcoholic KOH, the strong base (alkoxide) pulls off H from the carbon next to the C–X carbon.","This gives an alkene by elimination of HX.","In aqueous KOH, OH⁻ mostly acts as a nucleophile.","This gives an alcohol by substitution."],"others":"Swapping the two reagents reverses the facts, so alcohol with alcoholic KOH is wrong. Both giving an alkene ignores the nucleophile role in water. Both giving an alcohol ignores the strong-base role in alcohol.","trap":"Alcoholic gives alkene. Aqueous gives alcohol.","answer":"an alkene with alcoholic KOH, and an alcohol with aqueous KOH"},"chem-12-10-m21":{"idea":"Zaitsev's rule says the more substituted alkene is the major product of elimination.","steps":["With alcoholic KOH, 2-bromobutane loses HBr.","H can leave from carbon 1 or carbon 3.","Loss from carbon 3 gives but-2-ene, with two alkyl groups on the C=C.","This alkene is more stable, so it is the major product."],"others":"Butan-2-ol is a substitution product, which needs aqueous KOH. Butane would need a reduction, not loss of HX. But-1-ene is the minor product, with fewer alkyl groups on the double bond.","trap":"The H comes off the carbon with fewer hydrogens.","answer":"but-2-ene, the more highly substituted alkene"},"chem-12-10-m22":{"idea":"Grignard reagents are strong bases and react with water.","steps":["The reaction is R–X + Mg → R–Mg–X.","It is done in dry diethyl ether, which also stabilises the reagent.","Water would give R–H, an alkane, and destroy the reagent.","So every trace of water must be absent."],"others":"Water destroys the Grignard reagent, so water is wrong. Liquid ammonia and dilute acid would also react with it. Magnesium does not need a polar solvent for this.","trap":"Dry ether is a must.","answer":"in dry ether, as water would destroy the reagent"},"chem-12-10-m23":{"idea":"Chlorobenzene has a stronger C–Cl bond than ethyl chloride because of resonance and hybridisation.","steps":["The lone pair on Cl overlaps with the ring and shares electrons.","This gives the C–Cl bond partial double bond character.","The carbon is sp², which holds the bond more tightly than sp³.","So the bond is shorter and stronger and hard to break."],"others":"The bond is shorter and stronger, not longer and weaker. The carbon is sp², not sp³. Chlorobenzene is polar, so a non-polar molecule is wrong.","trap":"Short, strong bond, not a long, weak one.","answer":"the C–Cl bond has a partial double bond character and the carbon is sp² hybridised"},"chem-12-10-m24":{"idea":"A halogen on a benzene ring is unusual, as it deactivates the ring but still directs ortho and para.","steps":["Cl pulls electrons by the inductive effect, so the ring is less reactive.","Its lone pairs help stabilise the intermediate for ortho and para attack.","So the new group goes to the ortho and para positions.","The ring is deactivated overall."],"others":"Activating the ring is wrong, since the inductive pull wins on rate. Meta direction is not true for halogens. Meta and deactivating describes groups like –NO₂.","trap":"Halogens: deactivating but ortho, para directing.","answer":"directs the new group to the ortho and para positions, and it deactivates the ring"},"chem-12-10-m25":{"idea":"Chloroform is oxidised slowly by air in light to a toxic gas.","steps":["In light and air: 2CHCl₃ + O₂ → 2COCl₂ + 2HCl.","COCl₂ is phosgene, which is very poisonous.","A brown bottle blocks light, and a full bottle leaves little air.","A small amount of ethanol is often added to remove phosgene."],"others":"CCl₄ is not formed by this air oxidation. Methane is not a product. Chloroform does not attack glass.","trap":"Dark bottle stops light. Full bottle stops air.","answer":"slowly changes into phosgene, COCl₂, which is very poisonous"},"chem-12-10-m26":{"idea":"Some old solvents are now avoided because of health and environment harm.","steps":["CCl₄ vapour damages the liver, kidneys and nervous system.","It rises into the upper atmosphere.","There it takes part in the breakdown of ozone.","So its use has been stopped or limited."],"others":"CCl₄ does not dissolve in water. It does not burn, which is why it was used in extinguishers. It does not react with metal containers in any important way.","trap":"CCl₄ is not flammable, which is why it was used for fires.","answer":"it can damage the liver and it harms the ozone layer"},"chem-12-10-m27":{"idea":"In SN1, the nucleophile enters after the slow step, so the rate does not depend on it.","steps":["(CH₃)₃C–Br is tertiary, so it reacts by SN1.","Slow step: (CH₃)₃C–Br → (CH₃)₃C⁺ + Br⁻.","Rate = k[(CH₃)₃C–Br], with no OH⁻ term.","So doubling [OH⁻] leaves the rate unchanged."],"others":"OH⁻ does not make the halide less stable. OH⁻ is not in the slow step, so the rate is not doubled. The rate is not second order in OH⁻; the question says first order in the halide only.","trap":"Zero order in the nucleophile for SN1.","answer":"remains the same, since the slow step does not involve OH⁻"},"chem-12-10-n01":{"idea":"In a rate law, each concentration multiplies the rate by its own factor.","steps":["Rate = k[RX][Nu⁻].","[RX] goes ×2 and [Nu⁻] goes ×3.","New rate = k(2[RX])(3[Nu⁻]) = 6 × old rate.","The rate increases 6 times."],"trap":"Each factor multiplies; do not add them.","answer":"6"},"chem-12-10-n02":{"idea":"A first-order reaction has a half-life that does not depend on concentration.","steps":["t½ = 0.693 / k.","k = 3.0 × 10⁻³ s⁻¹.","t½ = 0.693 / (3.0 × 10⁻³) = 231 s.","Check: the unit of k is s⁻¹, so t½ comes out in seconds."],"answer":"231 s"},"chem-12-10-n03":{"idea":"For first order, the fraction left is e raised to minus kt.","steps":["[RX]/[RX]₀ = e^(−kt).","Time t = 10 min = 600 s.","kt = 3.0 × 10⁻³ × 600 = 1.8.","Fraction = e^(−1.8) = 0.1653."],"trap":"Convert minutes to seconds first, as k is per second.","answer":"0.1653"},"chem-12-10-n04":{"idea":"Percentage by mass is the mass of the element divided by the molar mass, times 100.","steps":["Molar mass of CHCl₃ = 12 + 1 + 3 × 35.5 = 119.5 g/mol.","Mass of Cl in one mole = 3 × 35.5 = 106.5 g.","Percentage = 106.5 / 119.5 × 100 = 89.12.","Check: C and H are 13 / 119.5 = 10.88 percent, and 100 − 10.88 = 89.12."],"answer":"89.12 %"},"chem-12-10-n05":{"idea":"In the Carius method, all the Cl ends up in AgCl, so its mass tells us the Cl mass.","steps":["Molar mass of AgCl = 108 + 35.5 = 143.5 g/mol.","Mass of Cl = 35.5 / 143.5 × 0.574 = 0.142 g.","Percentage of Cl = 0.142 / 0.300 × 100 = 47.33.","Check: 0.574 g is 0.0040 mol of AgCl, so 0.0040 mol Cl, which is 0.142 g."],"answer":"47.33 %"},"chem-12-10-n06":{"idea":"Find the theoretical yield from the mole ratio, then apply the percent yield.","steps":["Reaction: C₂H₅OH → C₂H₅Cl, 1 mol to 1 mol.","Moles of ethanol = 4.6 / 46 = 0.10 mol.","Theoretical mass = 0.10 × 64.5 = 6.45 g.","Actual mass = 6.45 × 0.80 = 5.16 g."],"trap":"Multiply by the yield only at the end.","answer":"5.16 g"},"chem-12-10-n07":{"idea":"Observed rotation depends on the excess of one enantiomer, not the total.","steps":["Excess of (+) form = 75 − 25 = 50 percent, or 0.50.","Observed rotation = [α] × l × c × 0.50.","= 66.5 × 2.0 × 0.20 × 0.50 = +13.3°.","Check: the (+) part gives +19.95° and the (−) part gives −6.65°, and the sum is +13.3°."],"trap":"The (−) enantiomer cancels part of the (+) rotation.","answer":"13.3°"},"chem-12-10-n08":{"idea":"With n different chiral carbons and no symmetry, there are 2ⁿ stereoisomers.","steps":["Each chiral carbon can be R or S.","The centres are independent.","Number of stereoisomers = 2ⁿ = 2³ = 8.","They make 4 pairs of enantiomers."],"answer":"8"}};
